<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/foundational/how-historians-work">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    History is not the past — it is <strong>the disciplined study of the past through its
    surviving evidence</strong>. The distinction matters more than it first appears. The past is
    gone; nobody can observe the signing of the Treaty of Versailles or hear the crowd at the
    sermons of Wycliffe as it happened. What remains is fragments: documents, artefacts,
    photographs, recordings — each one produced by someone, for a purpose, and each one surviving
    by accident or by the decisions of archivists who thought it worth keeping. The historian's
    craft is turning those fragments into justified claims about what happened and why.
  </p>
  <p>
    A useful analogy is the courtroom. A jury never saw the crime; it reasons from testimony,
    exhibits and expert analysis, and it is taught to distrust exactly the things that seem most
    convincing — a confident eyewitness, a vivid story. Historians work the same way: everything
    stands on <strong>sources</strong>, sources are interrogated rather than trusted, and single
    pieces of evidence are checked against each other before any conclusion is drawn. What
    separates history from myth or memory is precisely this method — the reasoning is exposed
    where others can check and challenge it. That is also why history keeps being rewritten: not
    because the past changed, but because new evidence surfaces, or old evidence gets looked at
    in a new way.
  </p>
  <p>
    The widget below traces the working loop: gather raw material, criticise it, corroborate it,
    and then interpret it with the frame chosen openly.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four-step method from artefact to argument" widget="equationStep" config={histCfg} />

  <h2>Primary and secondary sources</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>primary source</strong> is evidence produced <em>at or near the time</em> being
    studied, by people with direct connection to the events: a soldier's letter from the Somme, a
    parliamentary debate transcript, a census return, a coin, a medieval manor's account roll, a
    photograph, a recorded interview with a survivor. A <strong>secondary source</strong> is
    produced later, by someone interpreting primary material — a textbook, a biography, the very
    page you are reading. The distinction is about proximity and role, not age: a 1960s memoir
    about 1916 is a primary source <em>for 1960s memory</em> and a secondary source <em>for
    1916</em>. Historians ultimately stand on primary evidence; secondary sources are how they
    find it, and how they position their own argument against other historians'.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each type has characteristic strengths and weaknesses. Primary sources give direct access but
    were never written for you: a tax record was written to collect revenue, not to describe the
    economy. Secondary sources give synthesis and context but embed someone else's selection and
    interpretation, so they must themselves be checked — who wrote this, when, and with what
    evidence? There is also an in-between class, <strong>tertiary</strong> compilations like
    encyclopedias: useful for orientation, but not citable as evidence in serious work. A good
    working rule: every factual claim in an argument should trace back, through as few links as
    possible, to primary material or to a scholarly secondary work that itself cites it.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "primary" does not mean "reliable" and "secondary" does not
  mean "unreliable". A diary written the evening of an event is primary but may be self-serving;
  a modern historian's careful statistical reconstruction is secondary but may be far more
  accurate about the overall pattern. Reliability is judged source by source, never by type
  alone.</p>

  <h2>Source criticism: interrogating the witness</h2>
  <p>
    Before using any source, historians ask a battery of questions, traditionally split in two.
    <strong>External criticism</strong> asks: is it genuine? Is this letter really signed by
    Lincoln, is this "medieval" document a later forgery, do the paper, ink, language and
    handwriting fit its claimed date? (The <em>Donation of Constantine</em>, which seemed to
    grant the pope vast temporal power, was exposed as an eighth-century forgery partly by
    linguistic analysis — its Latin looked nothing like fourth-century usage.)
    <strong>Internal criticism</strong> asks: what does it actually mean and how far can it be
    trusted? Who wrote it, for what audience, with what purpose, and with what access to the
    facts? A royal proclamation, a soldier's letter and a merchant's ledger each tell the truth
    about different things: the proclamation tells you what the state wanted its subjects to
    believe, the letter what one literate man chose to tell his family, the ledger what someone
    counted carefully because money depended on it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Purpose is the master question. Every source is an <em>artifact of an intention</em>.
    Propaganda posters are honest about being propaganda, but the subtle cases matter more: a
    victory memoir downplays failures, a court chronicle flatters its patron, even a plain
    photograph involves choices — framing, timing, what was left outside the frame. Asking "why
    does this source exist?" converts a passive quotation into evidence.
  </p>

  <h2>Bias, perspective and reading against the grain</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Bias</strong> in everyday speech means prejudice; in source work it is broader —
    every source has a <em>position</em>, a point of view with blind spots. The historian's aim
    is not to find unbiased sources (there are none) but to <em>understand each source's
    position</em> well enough to compensate for it. Two techniques do most of the work. First,
    <strong>corroboration</strong>: one source is a claim; independent sources agreeing move it
    toward fact. Where sources <em>conflict</em> is often where the interesting history lives —
    whose account of the same battle differs, and why? Second, <strong>reading against the
    grain</strong>: extracting information the source never intended to give. The tax rolls that
    never name the peasants still reveal, in aggregate, how many households there were, what they
    owed, and when famine thinned the numbers. Silences are evidence too: the history of the
    enslaved, of women, of the illiterate majority must often be reconstructed from the records
    of the people who owned, governed and wrote about them — which is itself a fact about power.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a biased source cannot be used. In fact a biased source can
  be <em>perfect</em> evidence — of the bias. Wartime propaganda films tell you little about
  accurate casualty statistics and a great deal about how a government wanted its people to see
  the war, which is a legitimate and important historical question. The sin is not using a
  biased source; it is using one without saying what for.</p>

  <h2>Arguing from evidence: causation, not chronology</h2>
  <p>
    History's central intellectual act is the <strong>causal argument</strong>: not just what
    happened, but why. And its central discipline is that <strong>"earlier than" is not "because
    of"</strong>. The Latin tag is <em>post hoc ergo propter hoc</em> — "after this, therefore
    because of this" — and resisting it is the field's original sin and its ever-present
    temptation. The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand in June 1914 preceded the First
    World War, but saying it "caused" the war needs an argument: through what mechanism, given
    which other conditions? Historians therefore distinguish the <strong>trigger</strong> (the
    spark), the <strong>preconditions</strong> (long-term tensions that made a spark dangerous)
    and <strong>contingency</strong> (the role of decisions and chance along the way). A good
    causal argument weighs these explicitly and considers <em>counterfactuals</em>: if the
    alliance system had not existed, would the Sarajevo shots still have produced a general
    European war?
  </p>
  <p>
    Interpretation enters inevitably, because <strong>evidence underdetermines conclusions</strong>.
    The same archive can support an economic explanation of the French Revolution or a political
    one; the data rarely force a single verdict. Honest historians therefore state their frame —
    economic, cultural, political, "history from below" — and argue for it. This is what
    <strong>historiography</strong> means: the study of how interpretations have changed, from
    the "Great Man" narratives of the nineteenth century, through Marxist emphases on economic
    structures, to today's cultural and global turns. "The facts speak for themselves" is the
    first warning sign of bad history; facts speak only when interrogated.
  </p>

  <h2>Reliability, utility and provisional conclusions</h2>
  <p>
    Two evaluative axes are often confused, and keeping them apart dissolves half of all weak
    source analysis. <strong>Reliability</strong> asks: can this source be believed about what it
    claims? A physician's 1348 account of plague symptoms is highly reliable on symptoms (he saw
    them), far less so on causes (he blamed a celestial conjunction).
    <strong>Utility</strong> asks: how useful is this source for <em>this</em> question? The same
    unreliable chronicle may be superb evidence of how its author's monastery wished to be
    remembered. Utility depends on the question; reliability does not. The same distinction
    explains why "limited value" is never a complete judgement of a source — limited value
    <em>for what?</em>
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, every historical conclusion is <strong>provisional</strong> — not as a humble
    gesture but for a hard reason: new evidence can always appear. The opening of Soviet archives
    after 1991 rewrote scholarship on the early Cold War within a decade; the recovery of the
    <em>Vindolanda</em> writing tablets transformed knowledge of ordinary Roman frontier life
    from almost nothing to remarkable detail. Historians also increasingly work with sources
    earlier generations ignored: oral history, archaeobotany, shipwrecks, ice cores. A historian
    certain of everything has stopped doing history; the discipline's confidence lies in its
    method, not its conclusions.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Two philosophical problems sit underneath the craft. First, <strong>presentism</strong>:
    judging the past by present values. Sometimes it is unavoidable and right — no one should
    describe slavery in neutral administrative language. But presentism can also blind: reading
    ancient motives through modern categories (was Alexander "imperialist" in our sense?)
    invents anachronistic minds. The working compromise most historians adopt:
    <em>understand</em> the past on its own terms, and <em>evaluate</em> it where a shared moral
    floor exists, while saying openly which you are doing. Second, the <strong>objectivity
    debate</strong>: E. H. Carr's 1961 <em>What Is History?</em> argued that the historian is a
    product of their own society, so pure objectivity is a myth — yet Carr still insisted facts
    constrain, that some accounts are better supported than others. The modern settlement holds
    that line: interpretations are frameworks, evidence is the court, and the difference between
    history and propaganda is that history can lose arguments.
  </p>
  <p>
    Method at the frontier is increasingly quantitative and scientific. <em>Prosopography</em>
    reconstructs the lives of groups from scattered records; <em>climate history</em> reads tree
    rings and ice cores to connect harvest failures to revolts; digital corpora let scholars
    track the diffusion of a single idea across thousands of pamphlets. None of it escapes the
    old loop in the widget: source, criticism, corroboration, openly stated interpretation. The
    tools change; the discipline is the loop.
  </p>
  <p>
    One worked demonstration binds the whole chapter together, because it uses every tool at
    once. Consider the claim "medieval people believed the Earth was flat". A student of the
    method starts asking the source questions immediately. Who said this, and when? The belief
    turns out to be a nineteenth-century invention — popularised by Washington Irving's
    fictionalised biography of Columbus (1828) and by the Draper–White "conflict" narrative of
    science and religion — reading a modern prejudice back onto the past, which is textbook
    presentism. What did the period itself say? Primary sources say the opposite: Bede writes of
    the round Earth in the eighth century, the spherical Earth is standard university doctrine
    through the Middle Ages, and Columbus's dispute with his critics was over the Earth's
    <em>size</em>, not its shape — his critics, on this point, were closer to correct. How did
    the error survive? By circulation without criticism: an anecdote repeated in textbooks is a
    secondary source quoting a secondary source, the exact failure mode corroboration exists to
    catch. The flat-earth myth is a small thing, and that is the point — a method that catches
    it is worth trusting with the larger questions, from the causes of wars to the everyday
    lives the archives barely recorded.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Carr, What Is History? (1961); Gaddis, The Landscape of History (2002); Howell &amp; Prevenier, From Reliable Sources (2001); Tosh, The Pursuit of History; Russell, Inventing the Flat Earth (1991).</p>
